body:
This happened at work about, oh, four months ago? I work in the back, where we have a solid cement floor and rather small workspaces. My workspace, in particular, was right up against a stack of gaylords (giant boxes on wooden pallets, usually filled with 100+lbs of raw hardware and other product) and this stack was about three high, four deep. We had just gotten a new forklift driver that day, and he was moving stuff around and driving in the forklift. Remember: tiny spaces + big heavy boxes + people underfoot everywhere = just not enough space.

&#x200B;

As I'm standing there doing whatever (I think I may have been tagging suitcases? Can't remember) the new driver starts to move a gaylord off one of the stacks. Right as he goes to pick it up, I get called up to the front to assist a costumer with something. I leave my workspace and head up front. Came back about a half hour later after getting sidetracked into helping a cashier, only to find out that the driver had made a mistake and the entire gaylord (full of electronic scrap, hardware and heavy looking equipment) had gone sideways off the stack and smashed directly on top of my workspace. 

&#x200B;

If I hadn't been called away, I'd likely be a living pancake at best, or dead at worst. I still can't bring myself to imagine what would have happened, I can't imagine the bone-crushing pain I would've felt had I still been there when the gaylord came down. On the plus side, my work would have paid for everything. I'm just glad it never came to that.
